---
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How many cubic yards of rock do I need?",
    answer: "Multiply your area in square feet by the depth in feet, then divide by 27 (since there are 27 cubic feet in a cubic yard). For example, a 200 sq ft area at 3 inches deep: (200 x 0.25) / 27 = 1.85 cubic yards. Our calculator does this automatically for any shape and depth, including a waste factor for settling."
  },
  {
    question: "How much does a cubic yard of rock weigh?",
    answer: "Weight varies by material. Pea gravel and river rock average about 2,700 lbs (1.35 tons) per cubic yard. Crushed granite is heavier at 2,800 lbs (1.40 tons). Lava rock is much lighter at 1,500 lbs (0.75 tons) per cubic yard. Our calculator uses real quarry densities for each of the 12 rock types."
  },
  {
    question: "How deep should landscape rock be?",
    answer: "Recommended minimum depth depends on the rock size. Pea gravel and small crushed stone work well at 2 inches deep. River rock (1-3 inches) should be at least 3 inches deep. Large river rock (3-5 inches) needs 4 inches minimum. Rip rap and armor stone require 6 inches or more. Our calculator warns you if your selected depth is below the recommended minimum for your chosen material."
  },
  {
    question: "How many bags of rock do I need?",
    answer: "Standard rock bags at home improvement stores hold 0.5 cubic feet. To find the number of bags, calculate total cubic feet needed (area x depth in feet, plus waste factor), then divide by 0.5. For bulk orders, consider Super Sacks (about 35 cubic feet) or bulk delivery by the cubic yard."
  },
  {
    question: "What is the difference between cubic yards and tons?",
    answer: "Cubic yards measure volume (how much space the rock fills), while tons measure weight. The relationship depends on the rock density. Most landscaping aggregates weigh between 1.25 and 1.40 tons per cubic yard. Quarries and suppliers may sell by the cubic yard (volume) or by the ton (weight), so knowing both measurements helps you compare prices accurately."
  },
  {
    question: "Should I add extra rock for waste and settling?",
    answer: "Yes. A 10% waste factor is standard for most landscape rock projects. This accounts for settling, compaction, material lost during installation, and minor measurement variations. For base materials like quarry process that compact significantly, consider 15-20%. Our calculator lets you choose 5%, 10%, 15%, or 20% waste factors."
  },
  {
    question: "How do I calculate rock for a circular area?",
    answer: "Use the formula: Area = pi x radius x radius. Measure the radius (half the diameter) of your circular bed. Our calculator handles circles, rings (donut shapes, like around a fire pit), and four other shapes. Simply select the Circle tab, enter your radius, and the calculator does the rest."
  },
  {
    question: "What is the most cost-effective way to buy rock?",
    answer: "For small projects under 1 cubic yard, bagged rock from a home center may be convenient but costs 3-5x more per unit. For anything over 1 cubic yard, bulk delivery from a local quarry or landscape supply yard is far more economical. Most quarries deliver by the load (typically 10 cubic yards per dump truck). Our calculator shows you the breakdown in bags, Super Sacks, and truck loads so you can compare options."
  }
];
---
<section class="section" id="faq">
  <div class="container container-narrow">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="section-divider"></div>
      <p>Common questions about calculating rock coverage for landscaping and construction projects.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map((faq, i) => (
        <div class="faq-item animate-fade-in-up" style={`animation-delay: ${i * 0.05}s`}>
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>{faq.question}</span>
            <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
            </svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>{faq.answer}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ Schema -->
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": faqs.map(faq => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": faq.question,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": faq.answer
    }
  }))
})} />

<style>
  .faq-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .faq-item {
    background: var(--surface-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--surface-glass-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    overflow: hidden;
    transition: border-color 200ms ease;
  }

  .faq-item:hover {
    border-color: var(--slate-600);
  }

  .faq-item.open {
    border-color: var(--gold-500);
  }

  .faq-question {
    width: 100%;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-6);
    font-size: 0.925rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    text-align: left;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: color 150ms ease;
    background: none;
    border: none;
  }

  .faq-question:hover {
    color: var(--gold-400);
  }

  .faq-chevron {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    transition: transform 300ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1), color 150ms ease;
  }

  .faq-item.open .faq-chevron {
    transform: rotate(180deg);
    color: var(--gold-400);
  }

  .faq-answer {
    max-height: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    transition: max-height 400ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1), padding 400ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1);
    padding: 0 var(--space-6);
  }

  .faq-item.open .faq-answer {
    max-height: 400px;
    padding: 0 var(--space-6) var(--space-6);
  }

  .faq-answer p {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.8;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--surface-glass-border);
    padding-top: var(--space-4);
  }
</style>
